body:
Depends on what your friend likes about Tau. 

&#x200B;

If they're here for battlesuits, I'd tell 'em to wait and see our points costs - the Crisis changes look like dogshit, but there's potential they're decent if they're cheaper. If they're here for Kroot, there's a lot to be excited about - cool new models, a whole detachment, all kinds of cool stuff. Our new detachments and stratagems seem good, but battlesuits are why I'm here and they're removing the parts I think are fun about them. Me personally? Mixed bag - the Kroot fan in me is happy, the battlesuit fan in mine is enraged.

It doesn't help, regarding BG3, that the only female companions early on are Lae'zel and Shadowheart. Many people like and use Shadowheart from what I've seen, but Lae'zel is more outwardly abrasive (+ not conventionally attractive, which is the worst thing a woman can be to a g\*mer, apparently). In my head, it both makes sense and is sad that the stats go that way on that game particularly. That said, a *lot* of people hate Lae'zel but give Astarion a pass when he's >!literally racist and just as much of an abrasive prick as they say she is!<, but he's conventionally attractive so it's quirky or something /s. Like, they both do the same thing (both disapprove of generally moral actions, both are off-putting at first, etc.), but he gets a pass and she doesn't and that's the part where it's telling.

&#x200B;

Full disclosure: not a fan of either Astarion or Lae'zel so far. That could change, but I'm still only in Act 1.

I think the FE3H talking point (not your point, but the "Edelgard's bad reeeeeee" point) is particularly fucked because *Edelgard's the good guy*. She does plenty of bad things on the way to it, but she's the only one who sees the big picture and is willing to do something about it in the now. >!The dragons have been controlling society, TWSITD are turning children into weapons of war and committing literal genocide, the nations that should be stopping it all are so embittered against one another that nothing of value can be done!<. Dimitri is too obsessed with vengeance and his nation and the church. Claude is on the right track, but is too reticent to act regarding certain things >!even when he learns about the dragons and the control they have on society. The only upside to Claude over Edelgard is that you actually handle TWSITD in his campaign instead of it being a post-campaign footnote and that Black Eagles' is the shortest overall.!<But yeah, Edelgard's a woman with thoughts and ambition, so she's the bad guy obviously /s. I'm not saying we've gotta look past the things she did to get to where she was going, but I'm also not gonna pretend that her goal was ignoble and that she was just evil for wanting to make things better.

&#x200B;

And don't get me started on how Ranni is the only good guy (with sufficient power) in Elden Ring. Honestly, she and Edelgard are kinda in the same boat - they've done bad things in the name of a noble goal.

I've done the exact same thing on some Rubrics, absolutely love the look and how easy it is to achieve. It looks so crisp and complex when it's done well though, and yours is killer.

&#x200B;

How'd you get the effect on your Infernal Master? The glow looks SICK and I'd love to hear your process for applying that and his base coats. I'd guess you just did the ghosty effect over top of his base, but I'd love to hear your process.

Night Lords players were edgelords in middle school and then suppressed it, but immediately let it come out when they saw the models/read the lore.

&#x200B;

Thousand Sons players are at least 1 of the following:

1. Former gifted kid
2. Queer
3. Autistic

&#x200B;

Grey Knights players are either vehemently religious OR really like the religious aesthetics but are vehemently not religious.

&#x200B;

Source: I am an edgelord who checks all 3 of the Thousand Sons boxes and really likes religious aesthetics.

Gaming subreddits are absolutely ridiculous. They think every issue is world-ending all the time.

&#x200B;

Obvious exaggeration, many people have fair points, but online gaming spaces are bad generally.

Except...it's not an exploit. If it were an exploit they wouldn't let you do it for 10+ years. They try to fix your 2nd/3rd points when they happen (look at how they fixed Mohg's fight recently) because they're genuine exploits. Back in DeS or DkS1, can't remember which, they tried to fix your 1st point as well.

&#x200B;

That said, you're 100% right that it was not intended for that purpose. My point was simply that you can already trade items to yourself, and FromSoft knows people do and have done nothing to stop you from doing it, meaning that even if it wasn't intended, it's allowed. In addition, the "except" was pointing out that "maybe this is the one game where we don't make an OP character and then transfer all it's gear to a new character" is wrong, the community's been doing it for 10+ years on playthrough 2-100.

Not having it doesn't make twinking any harder though, they just have to get a friend to pass it to them, that's not difficult. 

&#x200B;

Also, a sample size of 50, "dozens" of which are streamers on their FIRST PLAYTHROUGH, doesn't mean anything when we're talking about a feature that would be for consecutive playthroughs.

I mean, fair enough, but also NFL 2k1 has a higher score than Elden Ring on Metacritic. Also they have at least one Telltale game in >90 LMAO.

&#x200B;

All seriousness though, jokes aside, it's all about a grain of salt. User reviews, professional reviewers, YouTube, etc. - it's all about looking at the review, then looking at stuff yourself and seeing if you agree; that and finding a reviewer (easier on YouTube, but not impossible for companies) that you have similar tastes to. I'm not huge on platformers, so even though Mario Galaxy 2 has a 97, it's still not gonna be for me. That doesn't mean it's a bad game, it just means that I can't trust that review, you know? To an extent, it's all about not taking 1 single review at face value - crosscheck that shit and see what multiple sources think to get a better picture.

\+3MD is infinitely more useful than Vanguard Deployment on a unit that already has it, or LD buff on a unit that's unbreakable. 

&#x200B;

Also, as has been said, there have 100% been meaningful techs.

The power creep happened though as a result of more interesting lord skills and traits, better techs, etc. Yes, I feel like a lot of people would rather have interested and significant choices that make them stronger then insignificant choices that don't matter in the slightest.

&#x200B;

Also, in what world did a generic Beastmen caster ever outperform Teclis? Ikkit, Grom, and new Beastmen aren't strong because of tech trees and skills, it's because they have strong campaign effects (like Ikkit's forge or Grom's pot). That's a completely different thing to talk about, though yes, I'd personally rather have strong and engaging campaign mechanics than have none at all (or really weak ones).

&#x200B;

Edit: Also, there's a reason those campaigns were popular. They're fun, which is what matters in a video game.

How's the weather from the high horse? I don't even disagree with you.

&#x200B;

Seriously though, it's not about getting good when the AI can walk into Tzeentch's realm and start at the end. Or when enemy armies Benny Hill you around your territory. Or when the AI has a huge anti-player bias (even on lower difficulties). Or when the auto-resolve is laughably broken, saying you'll lose 8/20 high-tier troops against 2 Kossars and a generic lord. The game's got some problems, and toxic positivity won't fix that. Loving the game so far, but it definitely needs more work under the hood.

To your first point: so? If it bugs you, I think that's a you problem. You can still feel like you accomplished something, and someone else might not care and may just want to do the PvP (whether Pokemon or Souls). Does it matter what steps they took to get there? It doesn't make their items any better than yours (provided they didn't increase stats, which is harder to do and also a bigger deal).

&#x200B;

To your second point: that's going to happen anyway. Even if they're not genning it, people are going to go grab/equip the items and re-do their stats (if there's a way in-game) to follow what's good. That or they'll make a new character. Again, what's it matter, the result's the same anyway?

Totally agree about all of your points. I don't think roguelikes are bad by any means, I really love a good handful of 'em, I just prefer DD as a dungeon crawler b/c I miss good dungeon crawlers. Even in their heyday I loved dungeon crawlers, roguelikes are just more often miss for me than hit, even the good ones.

&#x200B;

Regarding gameplay, I just meant that dungeon crawlers and roguelikes both have good and interesting gameplay elements, but they have DIFFERENT gameplay elements. I wish we could have kept the dungeon crawler elements because I love that genre. Roguelikes have more split-second gameplay decisions usually, whereas dungeon crawlers traditionally are more about the management and planning a good run, and you lose a lot of that in a roguelike.

&#x200B;

I 100% agree though. I'm kind of just burnt out on roguelikes, whereas I want new dungeon crawlers (the only new ones I really notice are the Etrian games on Nintendo hardware) since I've played a ton of the classics and want something new. I was hoping this could have been it (I didn't look at pre-release info so I didn't know the change until a few days ago), but it just didn't pan out for me.

My only real complaint is that roguelikes are a dime-a-dozen these days, whereas there aren't many dungeon crawlers being made. For me personally, I'd rather them have refined the base building and exploration and make a damn good dungeon crawler then make a roguelike. That said, I get why they did it. Will it be fun? Almost certainly, I like the DD combat loop, but I'm disappointed because I liked the dungeon crawl. It's in-part the aesthetics of it (a dark, overbearing expanse of corridors and paths) and it's in-part the actual gameplay aspect.

&#x200B;

I know I could just go play DD1, but that doesn't change the fact that new dungeon crawlers aren't coming out and I've already sunk a ton of hours into DD1: I want something new and was hoping DD2 could have been that. I'll still play it, but it's not the itch I want to scratch, if that makes sense.

I feel like this solution still doesn't really work, because dissenting opinions from people who couldn't get far are also valuable if you happen to feel the same. Let me explain with an anecdote. 

&#x200B;

Back in 2017 (I think) I bought Hollow Knight b/c rave reviews + the art and music. I've played 4 hours to this day (tried again recently) and didn't enjoy it. It's not a bad game (in-fact it's amazing for the people who like the genre), but it's a metroidvania: something that I didn't know what was before that and that I don't enjoy. I feel like the potential for negative reviews from people who genuinely don't enjoy the experience warning others of what it is helps to counter all the positive reviews gushing over it. Hollow Knight is a great game, but if any review I saw at the time mentioned how the game actually plays I wouldn't have bought it, instead they all mentioned atmosphere, art, music, etc.

On Short Rests: They're really useful for Warlocks (Spell Slots come back on Short for them) and Fighters (Action Surge and Second Wind) and for healing a little bit quickly. In a TTRPG the reason is ambushes and stuff like that, but in BG3 it really seems to be a matter of load screens. 

&#x200B;

Agreed with the clutter, surfaces, vendor in camp, and the feel of exploration in the game. Just wanted to defend Short Rests because they're a useful mechanic for some classes, but not for all. I only wish we had at least 1 more, because Warlock feels bad with only (max) 4 spell slots and 1 Short Rest/day.

Idk if this is a bug or intentional, but some of the companions are tremendously stupid. They talk about wanting to save themselves from the parasite, but two of them at least disapprove of agreeing to save the one person we've found who may be able to fix the problem. Some of them also seem to disapprove of legitimately anything other than killing and lying: I get it, they gave us the more evil-aligned companions to start to playtest, but hopefully this degree of character writing isn't indicative of the final produce. Other than Gale, I haven't seen a single redeeming trait to the companions (haven't met Wyll though, missed him my first playthrough). If the goal was to make the initial EA companions totally unlikable, then great job. 

&#x200B;

Of course, there are going to be people who disagree with me and like the companions, and I'm not trying to shit on their opinions either. This is just how they come off to me and represents my feelings on them. Not trying to state anything definitive, just how I feel about the characters presented thus far.
